SQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL, or SQL Server).<\\\\\\/li><li>A <strong>programming environment<\\\\\\/strong> (e.g., Java\\\\\\/Python\\\\\\/PHP if database connectivity is required).<\\\\\\/li><li>Computer with enough storage and memory to run database tools.<\\\\\\/li><\\\\\\/ul><p><strong>Skills Needed:<\\\\\\/strong><\\\\\\/p><ul><li>Problem-solving and logical thinking.<\\\\\\/li><li>Ability to design models (ER diagrams, schemas).<\\\\\\/li><li>Understanding of data organization and file handling.<\\\\\\/li><\\\\\\/ul>\\\"}\"}', '{\"en\":\"{\\\"en\\\":\\\"<p>After completing this course, students will be able to:<\\\\\\/p><ol><li><strong>Understand basic concepts<\\\\\\/strong> of databases, data models, and architectures.<\\\\\\/li><li><strong>Design relational databases<\\\\\\/strong> using ER (Entity-Relationship) diagrams.<\\\\\\/li><li><strong>Apply normalization techniques<\\\\\\/strong> to improve database efficiency.<\\\\\\/li><li><strong>Use SQL<\\\\\\/strong> for data definition, manipulation, and querying.<\\\\\\/li><li><strong>Develop database applications<\\\\\\/strong> for real-world problems.<\\\\\\/li><li><strong>Implement transaction management<\\\\\\/strong> (commit, rollback) for data integrity.<\\\\\\/li><li><strong>Apply security measures<\\\\\\/strong> like user roles and permissions.<\\\\\\/li><li><strong>Work with DBMS software<\\\\\\/strong> such as MySQL, Oracle, or SQL Server.<\\\\\\/li><\\\\\\/ol>\\\"}\"}', 1, NULL, 0, 1, 0, NULL, 0, 1, 1, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, NULL, 0, NULL, 0, 13, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL),
